<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>2.2.1.5 Domain, Range and Graph of function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A note on entering your answers: <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> are the logical words. <code>and</code> means the values satisfying both inequalities; <code>or</code> means the values satisfying at least one of them. Type <code>&lt;=</code> for \(\le\) and <code>&gt;=</code> for \(\ge\).</p>
<p>Consider the function given by \(f(x) = {@f@},\quad {@leftend@} \leq x \leq {@rightend@}\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>State the domain of the function. Give your answer as inequalities in \(x\), for example <code>x&gt;-1 and x&lt;=4</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]]</div></li>
<li><p>Complete the table of values for the function \( f(x) = {@f@} \) for \(x\) between \(-3\) and \(3\).</p>
<table class="tb-table">
<tbody>
<tr><th>input, \(x\)</th><th>output, \(f(x)\)</th></tr>
<tr><td>\(-3\)</td><td>\({@fatneg3@}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>\(-2\)</td><td>\({@fatneg2@}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>\(-1\)</td><td><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>\(0\)</td><td><div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>\(1\)</td><td><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>\(2\)</td><td><div>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>\(3\)</td><td>\({@fat3@}\)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></li>
<li><p>From the table of values, which graph shows \( f(x) = {@f@} \)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>Choose from the graphs below:</p>
<table>
  <tbody><tr>
    <td style="text-align: center;"><b>Graph A</b></td>
    <td style="text-align: center;"><b>Graph B</b></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align: center;">{@thumbnailplot(funcstoplot[1],"Graph A")@}</td>
    <td style="text-align: center;">{@thumbnailplot(funcstoplot[2],"Graph B")@}</td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td style="text-align: center;"><b>Graph C</b></td>
    <td style="text-align: center;"><b>Graph D</b></td>
  </tr><tr>
    <td style="text-align: center;">{@thumbnailplot(funcstoplot[3],"Graph C")@}</td>
    <td style="text-align: center;">{@thumbnailplot(funcstoplot[4],"Graph D")@}</td>
  </tr>
</tbody></table></li>
<li><p>Deduce the range of the function by inspecting the graph and table of values. Give your answer as inequalities in \(y\), for example <code>y&gt;=-2 and y&lt;5</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans7]] [[feedback:prt7]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>The <strong>domain</strong> is the set of inputs on which the function is defined. The formula \({@f@}\) could be evaluated for any real \(x\), but this function is defined only on the interval \({@leftend@} \leq x\leq {@rightend@}\), so that interval is the domain: \(-3 \le x\) and \(x \le 3\).</p></li>
<li><p>We fill in the table by substituting each value of \(x\) into \(f(x) = {@f@}\). Square first (the square of a negative number is positive), then multiply by \({@a@}\), then add \({@b@}\):</p>
<p>\(f(-1) = {@a@}(-1)^2 + {@b@} = {@ta2@}\),</p>
<p>\(f(0) = {@a@}(0)^2 + {@b@} = {@ta3@}\),</p>
<p>\(f(1) = {@a@}(1)^2 + {@b@} = {@ta4@}\) and</p>
<p>\(f(2) = {@a@}(2)^2 + {@b@} = {@ta5@}\).</p></li>
<li><p>The table is symmetric about \(x=0\), the smallest value is at \(x=0\), and the values grow on both sides: an upward-opening parabola that crosses the vertical axis at \({@b@}\). Pick the graph with these features.</p></li>
<li><p>The <strong>range</strong> is the set of output values. Because \(x^2 \ge 0\), the smallest value of \(f(x)\) occurs at \(x = 0\), which is \(f(0) = {@ta3@}\). Because the domain stops at \(x=\pm 3\), there is also a largest value, at \(x=-3\) and \(x=3\): \(f(3) = {@fat3@}\). Hence the range is \({@ta3@} \leq y\leq {@fat3@}\).</p></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
